I have a program in which there are about 10 tasks running at the same time. They are all calling the same function with different values, and I want to know how long it takes for each one of my tasks to execute this function. However, it seems to me that I can't use something like that :

A:=Clock;
MyFunction(...);
B:=Clock;
Time:= B-A;

Indeed, I think this would not return the "real" CPU Time, but the time elapsed between the beginning and the end of the function, which is not correct, because my tasks could be switched when they're executing the function. So, I'm wondering if there is a way to know the "real" CPU time of each one of my tasks, i.e. the time they really spent using the CPU ?

samples.general_completion:
I'm looking for a way to measure the CPU time of each individual task in a multithreaded program in Delphi. I've heard about the GetTickCount function, but I'm not sure if it's accurate enough for my needs. Is there a more precise way to do this in Delphi ?


You're correct that GetTickCount() won't give you the "real" CPU time because it measures the time between system ticks, which are not directly correlated to CPU time.

Delphi provides the TStopwatch class in the System.Diagnostics unit for this purpose. It allows you to measure the CPU time of a block of code accurately.

Here's an example:

```delphi
uses
  System.Diagnostics;

var
  stopwatch: TStopwatch;
begin
  stopwatch := TStopwatch.Create;
  try
    stopwatch.Start;
    // Your function here
    // stopwatch.Stop; // If you want to stop timing after the function call
    WriteLn('Function executed in: ', stopwatch.ElapsedMilliseconds);
  finally
    stopwatch.Free;
  end;
end;
```

This will give you the actual CPU time used by the function, including any time spent waiting for other threads to complete.

Note: TStopwatch measures elapsed time, not CPU

