Solution:

You can use the @PostConstruct annotation to validate the property after bean creation and throw an exception if it's empty. Here's how:

```java
@Component
public class Service {

  @Value("${service.property}")
  private String property;

  @PostConstruct
  public void validateProperty() {
    if (property == null || property.isEmpty()) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("Property 'property' is empty");
    }
  }
}
```

This way, you ensure that the property is set and not empty after bean creation, and if it is, an exception will be thrown. This approach requires no additional code during bean creation and uses the @PostConstruct annotation for validation, which fits your requirement for least written code and using annotations.

