You can achieve this by using a custom webpack loader. This loader will check if the file is a JSON file and if so, load it as JSON, otherwise, it will load it as a JavaScript module.

Here's an example of how you can create such a loader:

```javascript
const path = require('path');
const fs = require('fs');

module.exports = function (context, request, callback) {
  const filename = path.resolve(context.basePath, request);

  if (filename.endsWith('.json')) {
    fs.readFile(filename, 'utf8', (err, data) => {
      if (!err) {
        callback(null, JSON.parse(data));
      } else {
        callback(err);
      }
    });
  } else {
    callback(null, require(filename));
  }
};
```

This loader checks if the requested file ends with `.json`. If it does, it reads the file as JSON and parses it. Otherwise, it loads the file as a JavaScript module.

